      "writtenOn": "2018-11-15T20:50:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "No, I don\u0027t think this is a good idea.\n\nIn principle, you could say that a value \u003c\u003d 0 is legal and mean \"disabled\". You\u0027d have to document it in the comment for Settings.Global.CONSECUTIVE_DNS_TIMEOUT_THRESHOLD in Settings.java by saying \"set to 0 to disable\" or something similar.\n\nBut in this case, you already have a dedicated mechanism for enabling/disabling this specific data stall evaluation type. It does not make any sense, API-wise, to turn it on but give it a \u003c\u003d 0 value ; anyone who does that is definitely making a configuration error. So I think it makes more sense to throw IAE here.\n\nBut I also understand you\u0027re worried about gservices mistakenly writing a 0 value here and crashing the system server for everyone, and I think that\u0027s a valid concern. So I suggest you simply replace the call to Log.e() to a call to Log.wtf() here. That will not crash the system server, but it will make some alarms go off on our side and we\u0027ll know to push a fix.\nAlso do document in Settings.java, saying this value should be \u003e 0 if the DNS data stall detection is enabled.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1556,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 1556,
        "endChar": 79
      },
